WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.070
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I was

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:13.870
sitting in my bedroom wearing my comfiest
sweatpants slamming on my keyboard.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.180
I was working on this talk.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:23.010
Searching for the words for an
inspirational, hopefully not too preachy,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:29.060
call for all of us to work on bridging the
divides in American culture and politics.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.570
I was writing something like, each and
every one of us should be concerned about

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.820
the future of democracy,
when something had caught my eye.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.910
It was a framed picture on my
dresser of my college friends and

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.350
me at our graduation.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.449
This photo contains two Megans,
two Laurens, two Kates and

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:52.365
my best friend Andrea.

00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:57.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All of us grew up on the US coast.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:02.090
After college,
each of us moved back to the US coast.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:06.490
Almost all of us, with a few exceptions,
have gone to graduate school.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.530
We're living in the Bay Area,
Los Angeles, Boston, and

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:15.510
New York working in business,
medicine, and law, and staying

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:19.580
in touch with each other by emailing
around criticisms of Donald Trump.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.390
Looking at this picture,
it became so clear to me.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.850
Things like this are where
the national disconnects start.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:36.220
We as humans, seek out others who
are like us for close social connection.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.790
Psychologist called this homophily,

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.180
meaning birds of a feather,
flock together.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.540
Think of your closest friends.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.520
Are they similar to you and
each other and race and age?

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.710
Did you go to the same kinds of schools,
or

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.550
do you make roughly
the same amount of money?

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.690
Do you hold similar views on religion and
politics?

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.590
If so, this is completely natural.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.770
We do it for good reason.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.610
Groups whose members share the same
backgrounds and vocabularies and

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.300
purposes can form quickly and provide
their members with substantial support.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.430
For minority groups, bonding together
like this can be particularly valuable.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.900
Because it affirms a sense of identity,
and

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.900
increases collective resources
in the face of oppression.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.540
So some of this is good.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.510
But our society has overshot the mark.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.970
We live today with levels of
disconnectedness and division

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.550
that are not the result only of proverbial
birds of a feather flocking together, but

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.200
also of insidious, institution
level forces that have separated

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.800
us to the point where different Americans
often see each other as different species.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.200
For one, there's the news media,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.600
which allows us to hear
only one side of the story.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.500
I know I indulge this opportunity.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.190
Checking the New York Times
homepage multiple times per day but

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.539
never watching Fox News.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.150
Also we're divided by where we live,

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.090
segregated along both racial and
economic lines.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.355
This story is told by the Jefferson
Counties, Jefferson County Mississippi is

00:03:18.355 --> 00:03:23.340
86% Black,
Jefferson County Missouri is 95% White.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.650
Stanford Business School graduates
bee line to New York, and

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.860
San Francisco after graduation.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.530
Whereas the average American lives
within 18 miles of his, or her mom.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.820
And then there's income inequality, the
result of macro trends like globalization,

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.340
and automation.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.260
Which have put many out of work while
the highly educated have gotten richer.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.790
I'll be honest here that this problem has
taken on a new sense of importance and

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.940
personal urgency for me after this
year's presidential election.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.370
But why did it take
a presidential election for

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.630
me to get focused on
such an impactful issue?

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.800
This isn't just birds of
a feather flocking together.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.450
This is a problem of exposure.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:14.240
Americans today are divided,
economically, culturally, politically.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.460
And many of us have just passively
fallen into this pattern.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.240
The forces that divide us are so
powerful and so

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.810
pervasive that we've come to see disunity
as acceptable because it is inevitable.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.860
It is not inevitable.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:34.270
It's not inevitable because each
of us has the ability not only to

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.560
bond together with those who are like us,
but also to bridge with those who are not.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.560
We can resist the forces
that pull us apart

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.170
by forming personal relationships with
people who are different from us.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.520
And if we were to leave up to our ideals
of democracy, and diversity, we must.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.890
The case for bridging is strong.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:01.610
Everyone from the founding fathers to
today's political scientists have argued

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:06.430
that Americans need to form personal
relationships across demographic lines

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.540
in order to make democracy in
a heterogeneous country work.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.300
But you don't have to care about
democracy in a heterogeneous country.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.370
Bridging is simply good for us as people.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.630
It rounds up our perspective and

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.820
teaches us things that we
otherwise wouldn't learn.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:26.270
It makes us better business people
who really know our customers and

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:29.200
better policy makers who
really know our constituents.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.940
I believe many of us already
have the inclination

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.620
to put ourselves in someone else's shoes.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.930
But without shared experience,
do we really have the ability?

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:46.670
There are three simple
ways that we can bridge.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:50.260
First, recognize the diversity
within our own groups.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.270
Remember my college friends?

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:56.870
My best friend Andrea is a teacher
in inner-city charter schools.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:58.240
I used to be a banker.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.660
So while our friendship
represents bonding in many ways,

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.350
it's actually bridging along
this dimension of profession.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.690
I have learned so much from Andrea.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.570
Hearing about her students and
their lives.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.280
Befriending her coworkers
who are career educators.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.060
Visiting her schools in downtown New York,
New Jersey and Hunters Point,

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:17.600
San Francisco.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.350
These experiences have
given me an empathy for

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:25.070
the challenges that low income
students in urban environment face and

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:26.915
this issue is now
extremely important to me.

00:06:26.915 --> 00:06:32.544
We all have our Andreas who can show us
something outside of our usual routine.

00:06:34.687 --> 00:06:38.228
Second, let's change the way
we have conversations.

00:06:38.228 --> 00:06:43.340
We miss out on so many opportunities
to bridge because we try to argue and

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.210
convince rather than listen and
understand.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:52.750
Some of us here maybe guilty of this in
the last political argument that we had.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.132
My mom who's here today and
appears to be crying.

00:06:56.132 --> 00:07:01.549
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Has another way.

00:07:01.549 --> 00:07:06.050
[LAUGH] She is a world class chatter and

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:10.090
we joke in our family that if you leave
her alone and you go a short line for

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:14.320
even a few minutes she will know the life
story of the person sitting next to her

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.048
and the cashier but the time you get back.

00:07:16.048 --> 00:07:22.890
This daily practice prepares her well for
more meaningful bridging relationships.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:28.100
She was an effective advocate for
adolescents in the foster care system and

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:34.120
is a fantastic psychologist for mentally
ill individuals because she listens and

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.420
empathizes and keeps an open mind.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:41.560
My mom's interpersonal
relationships broaden her exposure,

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:46.470
giving her a well-tested yet
ever evolving view on the world.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:47.182
I aspire of that.

00:07:49.525 --> 00:07:54.365
Third, recognize the things in yourself
that might be different to someone

00:07:54.365 --> 00:07:56.340
else and find ways to share it.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.270
My Jewish friends are great at this.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.075
They've invited me to Shabbat dinners and
Yom Kippur breakfast.

00:08:04.075 --> 00:08:05.680
[LAUGH] And

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.230
these have been experiences to share in
their cultures that I have treasured.

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.080
If this sounds a little awkward or

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.080
uncomfortable to you, take heart in
the fact that Samantha Powers, the former

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.980
US representative to the United Nations
befriended her Russian counterpart and

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:26.860
frequent opponent by taking him to
theater performances in Central Park.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:31.990
Or Knicks games in Madison Square Garden,
when he was in New York for UN meetings.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.860
If she can manage to do this, we should be
excited to welcome others into our world.

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:40.470
These steps may seem small,
but that's the point.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:45.450
Our society can be transformed
through individual relationships.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:50.360
These relationships are ends in
of themselves, as well as means

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:55.409
to more informed and compassionate
policy making and business practices.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:01.841
The sooner we stop bridging, the sooner
this becomes a habit, even a way of life.

00:09:01.841 --> 00:09:08.020
Imagine the possibilities when each of
us applies our creativity and ingenuity

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:13.630
towards understanding people who look and
think and live differently from us.

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:17.138
With more of us building bridges,
the prospects for

00:09:17.138 --> 00:09:19.301
a more united future are great.

00:09:19.301 --> 00:09:20.025
Thank you.

00:09:20.025 --> 00:09:22.350
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.864
[MUSIC]

